\title{The Kernel/Game Bridge: A Unification of Stable Argumentation
Semantics, Digraph Kernels, and Combinatorial Game Solutions}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 14, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop a self-contained bridge connecting three theories usually studied in
isolation: the \emph{stable extension} semantics of abstract argumentation
frameworks, the theory of \emph{kernels} of directed graphs (independent,
absorbing vertex sets in the sense of von~Neumann and Morgenstern), and the
classification of positions of a combinatorial game into wins and losses. The
central identity is a single translation,
\[
\textbf{Stable}(R) \;=\; \textbf{Kernel}(\flip R) \;=\; \textbf{GameSolution}(\flip R),
\]
which holds with no hypotheses whatsoever on the underlying relation---no
finiteness, no well-foundedness. Building on this dictionary we prove three
substantive results. First, a \emph{normal-play} theorem: terminal positions are
forced to lie in every kernel, so the convention ``no move implies a loss'' is a
consequence of the kernel axioms rather than a stipulation. Second, an
\emph{obstruction} theorem: the directed $3$-cycle possesses no kernel, hence the
three-argument cyclic framework has no stable extension and the corresponding game
has no consistent win/loss labelling. Third, a \emph{determinacy} theorem: any
relation whose reverse is well-founded---equivalently, any game admitting no
infinite play---has a \emph{unique} kernel, computed by the standard game
recursion; consequently a well-founded argumentation framework has a unique stable
extension. The last statement simultaneously instantiates Zermelo's determinacy
theorem for terminating games, a Richardson-style kernel existence theorem for
well-founded digraphs, and a uniqueness theorem for well-founded argumentation
semantics.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Three research communities have, independently, converged on a single
mathematical object.

Abstract argumentation, introduced by Dung, models a debate as a set $A$ of
arguments together with an \emph{attack} relation $R$, where $R\,a\,b$ reads
``$a$ attacks $b$.'' Among the semantics for acceptable positions, the
\emph{stable} semantics is the most decisive: a stable extension is a
conflict-free set that attacks every argument outside it.

Directed graph theory, following von~Neumann and Morgenstern, studies
\emph{kernels}: a kernel of a digraph $D$ is a vertex set that is simultaneously
\emph{independent} (no internal edge) and \emph{absorbing} (every external vertex
has an edge into the set).

Combinatorial game theory classifies the positions of a two-player game with move
relation $M$ into P-positions (a loss for the player to move) and N-positions (a
win for the player about to move). Under normal play the P-positions form a
self-consistent labelling.

We make the connection precise, prove it needs no regularity hypotheses, and
harvest the consequences. The \emph{dictionary} is elementary; the \emph{payoff}
is that structural results become tri-lingual, provable once and read three ways.
Throughout, $A$ is an arbitrary type and relations are arbitrary binary relations.
For a relation $R$ we write $\flip R$ for its transpose, $(\flip R)\,a\,b
\iff R\,b\,a$.

\section{Definitions}

\subsection{Argumentation semantics}

\begin{definition}[Conflict-free]
A set $S \subseteq A$ is \emph{conflict-free} for the attack relation $R$ if no
member attacks another: $\forall a \in S,\ \forall b \in S,\ \neg\, R\,a\,b.$
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Stable extension]
A set $S \subseteq A$ is a \emph{stable extension} of $R$ if it is conflict-free
and attacks every outsider:
\[
\mathrm{Stable}(R,S)\ :\iff\ \mathrm{ConflictFree}(R,S)\ \wedge\
\bigl(\forall a \notin S,\ \exists b \in S,\ R\,b\,a\bigr).
\]
\end{definition}

\subsection{Digraph kernels}

\begin{definition}[Independent set]
$S \subseteq A$ is \emph{independent} in the digraph $D$ if no edge joins two of
its members: $\forall a \in S,\ \forall b \in S,\ \neg\, D\,a\,b.$
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Absorbing set]
$S \subseteq A$ is \emph{absorbing} in $D$ if every outside vertex has an edge into
$S$: $\forall a \notin S,\ \exists b \in S,\ D\,a\,b.$
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Kernel]
$S$ is a \emph{kernel} of $D$ if it is both independent and absorbing.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Combinatorial games}

\begin{definition}[Game solution]
Identifying a game with its move relation $M$ ($M\,p\,q$ meaning ``from $p$ a legal
move reaches $q$''), a set $P \subseteq A$ is a \emph{solution} (the set of
P-positions) of $M$ if $P$ is a kernel of the move digraph $M$: no move from a
P-position reaches a P-position, and from every non-P-position some move reaches a
P-position.
\end{definition}

\section{The dictionary}

\begin{theorem}[Argumentation--Graph Bridge]\label{thm:sk}
For every relation $R$ and set $S$, $\mathrm{Stable}(R,S) \iff
\mathrm{Kernel}(\flip R,\, S)$. No hypotheses on $A$ or $R$ are required.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Unfold both sides. Conflict-freeness of $S$ for $R$ is $\forall a,b \in S,\ \neg
R\,a\,b$; independence of $S$ for $\flip R$ is $\forall a,b\in S,\ \neg R\,b\,a$;
these agree after swapping the two bound variables. The decisiveness clause of
stability, $\forall a \notin S,\ \exists b \in S,\ R\,b\,a$, is verbatim the
absorbing clause $\forall a \notin S,\ \exists b\in S,\ (\flip R)\,a\,b$. Each
conjunct matches its counterpart.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Argumentation--Game Bridge]\label{thm:sg}
For every $R$ and $S$, $\mathrm{Stable}(R,S) \iff \mathrm{GameSolution}(\flip R,\, S)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from Theorem~\ref{thm:sk} and the definition of a game solution as a
kernel of the move relation.
\end{proof}

Combining, we obtain the master identity $\mathrm{Stable}(R,\cdot) =
\mathrm{Kernel}(\flip R,\cdot) = \mathrm{GameSolution}(\flip R,\cdot)$, valid for
arbitrary $R$.

\begin{theorem}[Terminal positions are losing]\label{thm:term}
Let $P$ be any kernel of a move relation $M$, and let $a$ be terminal, i.e.
$\forall b,\ \neg\, M\,a\,b$. Then $a \in P$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose $a \notin P$. By absorption there is $b \in P$ with $M\,a\,b$,
contradicting terminality. Hence $a \in P$.
\end{proof}

\section{The odd-cycle obstruction}

\begin{definition}[Directed $3$-cycle]
On $\{0,1,2\}$ define $\mathrm{cyc}_3\,a\,b :\iff b = a+1$ (indices mod $3$), i.e.
$0 \to 1 \to 2 \to 0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[No kernel]\label{thm:nok}
The digraph $\mathrm{cyc}_3$ has no kernel.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Check the eight subsets. The empty set is not absorbing. Each singleton fails
absorption: the vertex two steps back from its member has its only out-edge to a
different vertex and no in-edge from the singleton. Each two-element set contains an
edge (of any two of the three vertices, one is the successor of the other), so is
not independent; the full set likewise. Hence no subset is a kernel.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No stable extension]\label{thm:nos}
The framework $(\{0,1,2\}, \mathrm{cyc}_3)$ has no stable extension.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:sk} a stable extension would be a kernel of $\flip
(\mathrm{cyc}_3)$, again a directed $3$-cycle, which has none by
Theorem~\ref{thm:nok}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Theorems~\ref{thm:nok}--\ref{thm:nos} are one fact in three languages: a debate
with no verdict, a digraph with no von~Neumann--Morgenstern solution, and a game
with no consistent labelling. An odd directed cycle supports an infinite,
never-resolving chase, which is why a regularity hypothesis is unavoidable for
existence.
\end{remark}

\section{Well-founded existence, uniqueness, and determinacy}

We call the game $M$ \emph{well-founded} when $\flip M$ is a well-founded relation:
there is no infinite forward play $a_0 \to a_1 \to \cdots$.

\begin{definition}[Losing positions]
For a well-founded game $M$ define $\mathrm{isLoss}$ by well-founded recursion along
$\flip M$:
\[
\mathrm{isLoss}(a) \ :\iff\ \forall b,\ M\,a\,b \Rightarrow \neg\,\mathrm{isLoss}(b).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Fixed-point equation]\label{lem:fix}
For every $a$, $\mathrm{isLoss}(a) \iff \bigl(\forall b,\ M\,a\,b \Rightarrow
\neg\,\mathrm{isLoss}(b)\bigr)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
This is the unfolding equation of the well-founded recursion defining
$\mathrm{isLoss}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[P-positions form a kernel]\label{thm:kL}
For a well-founded game $M$, the set $L := \{ a \mid \mathrm{isLoss}(a)\}$ is a
kernel of $M$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
\emph{Independence.} If $a,b \in L$ and $M\,a\,b$, Lemma~\ref{lem:fix} at $a$ gives
$\neg\,\mathrm{isLoss}(b)$, contradicting $b \in L$. \emph{Absorption.} If $a
\notin L$, the negation of the defining clause yields $b$ with $M\,a\,b$ and
$\mathrm{isLoss}(b)$, i.e. a move into $L$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Kernel uniqueness]\label{thm:uniq}
If $M$ is well-founded and $S$ is any kernel of $M$, then $S = L$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Show $a \in S \iff \mathrm{isLoss}(a)$ by well-founded induction along $\flip M$;
assume the equivalence for all $b$ with $M\,a\,b$. ($\Rightarrow$) If $a \in S$ and
some move $M\,a\,b$ had $\mathrm{isLoss}(b)$, the hypothesis gives $b \in S$,
contradicting independence; so all moves land in non-losses and
$\mathrm{isLoss}(a)$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:fix}. ($\Leftarrow$) If $\mathrm{isLoss}(a)$
but $a \notin S$, absorption gives $b \in S$ with $M\,a\,b$; the hypothesis gives
$\mathrm{isLoss}(b)$, contradicting the defining clause at $a$. Hence $a \in S$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unique kernel]\label{thm:ek}
Every well-founded digraph $M$ has a unique kernel, namely $L$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Existence is Theorem~\ref{thm:kL}; uniqueness is Theorem~\ref{thm:uniq}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Zermelo determinacy]
Every well-founded game has a unique solution.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A game solution is a kernel, so this is Theorem~\ref{thm:ek}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unique stable extension]
If the attack relation $R$ is well-founded, then $(A,R)$ has a unique stable
extension.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Well-foundedness of $R$ is that of $\flip(\flip R)$, so $\flip R$ has a unique
kernel $S$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:ek}; by Theorem~\ref{thm:sk}, $S$ is the unique
stable extension of $R$.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{algorithm}[Kernel by game recursion]
For a finite acyclic move relation $M$: topologically sort so every move points to
an already-processed successor; process from sinks toward sources, labelling $a$
\textbf{loss} iff all its out-neighbours are labelled \textbf{win}; return the
loss-labelled set. Runs in $O(|V|+|E|)$ time.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Stable extension via transpose]
To compute the unique stable extension of a well-founded framework $(A,R)$: reverse
the edges to form $\flip R$ and run the previous algorithm. Correctness follows
from Theorems~\ref{thm:sk} and~\ref{thm:ek}.
\end{algorithm}

\section{Applications and discussion}

For a debate with no vicious cycles, the uniqueness theorem guarantees a single
computable verdict partitioning claims into accepted and defeated. In games, the
determinacy theorem is the formal backbone of retrograde analysis, giving the
unique P/N labelling of any terminating game. The odd-cycle obstruction is a
reusable diagnostic: any debate, network, or game resisting resolution must
contain an odd cyclic chase.

We stress the dichotomy: \emph{maximal} argumentation semantics exist
unconditionally, whereas \emph{stable} semantics need not, the triangle being the
minimal witness. Well-foundedness is exactly the hypothesis that repairs the
defect, and it also delivers uniqueness and a linear-time construction; in game
language it is precisely the ``no infinite play'' condition of Zermelo's theorem.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Even cycles and kernel-perfect digraphs.} Formalise the positive
side: even directed cycles have kernels, and more generally every digraph with no
odd directed cycle is kernel-perfect (Richardson's theorem).
\item \textbf{Grounded $=$ unique stable in the well-founded case.} Identify the
unique stable extension with the grounded extension (least fixed point of the
characteristic defence operator).
\item \textbf{Quantitative obstruction theory.} Characterise finite digraphs with
no kernel via their odd-cycle structure, with a certificate calculus for
non-existence.
\item \textbf{Weaker semantics.} Extend the dictionary to semi-stable and stage
semantics and their graph- and game-theoretic counterparts.
\end{itemize}

\end{document}

